WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.320
Adrenaline stays outside a liver cell.

2
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:05.420
How can enzymes inside respond?

3
00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:11.140
Watch the outer signal stop at the membrane while an inner signal begins moving.

4
00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:16.540
The answer must connect both spaces without carrying adrenaline across.

5
00:00:20.117 --> 00:00:23.717
The receptor changes shape and activates Gs on the inner face.

6
00:00:25.137 --> 00:00:27.937
Gs reaches adenylyl cyclase, in the membrane.

7
00:00:29.157 --> 00:00:31.817
The enzyme turns cytosolic ATP into cAMP.

8
00:00:33.017 --> 00:00:36.697
The membrane holds the first step beside the inner messenger is produced.

9
00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:40.410
cAMP is the second messenger here.

10
00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:45.830
Its level changes inside after the first messenger binds outside.

11
00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:51.990
Many copies can carry one receptor input toward intracellular effectors.

12
00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:57.450
Unlike adrenaline, these copies never need to cross the membrane from outside.

13
00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:03.710
The membrane step keeps making messenger while the receptor pathway remains active.

14
00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:09.930
Every gold token carries the same chemical information, so quantity becomes

15
00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:13.430
adjustable. More tokens raise the level.

16
00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:16.850
Their location and lifetime also matter.

17
00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:20.610
A source would let cAMP keep accumulating.

18
00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:24.070
Phosphodiesterase provides the opposing process.

19
00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:29.370
It opens cAMP's ring and leaves inactive 5′-AMP, erasing the message.

20
00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:34.290
That product cannot switch on the kinase that reads cAMP.

21
00:01:35.183 --> 00:01:40.363
When removal is proportional to the amount present, it strengthens as cAMP

22
00:01:40.363 --> 00:01:46.223
accumulates. A higher level makes encounters with the red enzyme more frequent.

23
00:01:47.503 --> 00:01:50.403
The level settles when synthesis and breakdown match.

24
00:01:51.683 --> 00:01:54.443
This curve is computed from that first-order model.

25
00:01:55.517 --> 00:02:00.157
Now keep the blue source unchanged and strengthen the red removal process.

26
00:02:01.497 --> 00:02:04.437
Nothing else in the cell changes during this comparison.

27
00:02:05.917 --> 00:02:10.197
Where will the cAMP level settle: above its old plateau, or below it?

28
00:02:16.083 --> 00:02:22.723
Below. At every cAMP level, stronger phosphodiesterase removes more.

29
00:02:24.003 --> 00:02:28.943
A lower concentration is enough for removal to match the unchanged source, so the

30
00:02:28.943 --> 00:02:30.363
computed plateau falls.

31
00:02:31.243 --> 00:02:33.943
The balance returns, but at a different level.

32
00:02:35.017 --> 00:02:39.497
Gs raises synthesis, while Gi lowers some forms of cyclase.

33
00:02:41.037 --> 00:02:42.737
Phosphodiesterases set removal.

34
00:02:44.117 --> 00:02:48.577
These controls change the messenger before any downstream target is chosen.

35
00:02:49.717 --> 00:02:53.897
When adrenaline leaves, the receptor stops activating new Gs.

36
00:02:55.117 --> 00:02:59.957
Active Gs lingers until it splits its GTP, so cyclase drive fades.

37
00:03:01.317 --> 00:03:03.557
Synthesis falls back to its resting rate.

38
00:03:05.097 --> 00:03:08.977
Phosphodiesterase then returns cAMP toward its resting level.

39
00:03:10.597 --> 00:03:12.777
Faster removal makes that return briefer.

40
00:03:12.777 --> 00:03:13.750
Phosphodiesterase then returns cyclic amp toward its resting level.

41
00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:17.330
The message therefore has an adjustable height and lifetime.

42
00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:20.870
Its amount is not the response itself.

43
00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:25.430
Several possible effectors wait in the same cytosol.

44
00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:31.810
The next question is how a cAMP reader turns that level into a specific action.
